// ora-mod-inv — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-inv-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
QB['ora-mod-inv'] = (QB['ora-mod-inv'] || []).concat([["Logistics","How would you architect transportation and global trade with Oracle?","- **OTM** for planning, tendering, carrier rates, freight settlement.\n- **GTM** for trade compliance: restricted party screening, classification (HTS/ECCN), licenses, customs filings, duty management.\n- **Integration**: Fusion OM/Shipping ↔ OTM/GTM (native integration), carriers via EDI/APIs, customs brokers.\n- **Landed cost** management feeding costing.\n- **Visibility**: shipment tracking feeds to customers.","D"],["Inventory","How would you design inventory visibility across Oracle and non-Oracle locations?","- **Fusion** holds on-hand for Oracle-managed orgs.\n- **External locations** (3PLs, consignment sites, stores) send snapshots/transactions into Fusion as external orgs or into a visibility layer.\n- **GOP** collects supply from all sources for promising.\n- **Control tower** dashboards for availability and aging.\n- Agreed **latency** and reconciliation process.","D"],["Inventory","How would you design an inventory optimization approach?","- **Segment** items (ABC/XYZ: value and variability).\n- Set **service level targets** by segment.\n- Calculate **safety stock** statistically (demand and lead-time variability).\n- Optimize **order quantities** (EOQ, lot sizes) vs ordering costs.\n- **Multi-echelon** thinking for DC networks (where to hold buffers).\n- Monitor **excess and obsolete** inventory; disposition rules.\n- Tools: Fusion Replenishment Planning or specialized optimization.","D"],["Returns","How would you architect reverse logistics at scale?","- **Return authorization** policies by product/customer; online return portal.\n- **Routing** returns to the right node (DC, repair center, supplier).\n- **Inspection and disposition** rules (restock, refurbish, recycle, scrap, return to vendor).\n- **Credit** timing rules (at authorization vs receipt vs inspection).\n- **Refurbishment** via work orders; serialized tracking.\n- Analytics on return reasons and cost.","D"],["Logistics","What is cross-docking and how would you support it?","**Cross-docking** moves inbound goods straight to outbound shipping with little or no storage.\n- Requires **visibility** of inbound supply (ASNs) and outbound demand.\n- **Planned cross-dock** (known at receipt) vs **opportunistic** (WMS decides).\n- Supported in WMS capabilities (Fusion Warehouse Management or best-of-breed).\n- Benefits: faster flow, lower storage cost. Needs tight timing and data accuracy.","D"],["SCM Basics","What modules make up Oracle Fusion SCM?","- **Product Management / Product Hub** — items and item structures.\n- **Procurement** — Purchasing, Self Service Procurement, Sourcing, Supplier Portal, Supplier Qualification.\n- **Inventory Management** — organizations, subinventories, transactions, counts.\n- **Order Management** — sales orders and orchestration; **Global Order Promising**.\n- **Logistics** — Shipping, Receiving, Transportation.\n- **Cost Management** — item cost, receipt accounting, cost accounting.\n- **Manufacturing** — work definitions, work orders.\n- **Supply Chain Planning** — demand, supply, replenishment.\n\nTip: Say which ones you've worked on and to what depth.","B"],["Inventory","What is the difference between an inventory organization, a subinventory and a locator?","- **Inventory organization** — a plant, warehouse or distribution center that holds items and inventory (has its own item attributes, costing and calendar).\n- **Subinventory** — a physical or logical area within the org (e.g. Stores, FG, Staging, MRB). Can be tracked (quantity on hand) or expense.\n- **Locator** — a specific bin/shelf inside a subinventory, e.g. Row.Rack.Bin.\n\nHierarchy: Organization → Subinventory → Locator.","B"],["Inventory","What is an item master organization and why do items exist in it?","- Items are created once in the **item master organization** (in Fusion, it's the master org in Product Hub) and then **assigned** to the inventory orgs that use them.\n- **Master-level attributes** must be the same everywhere (e.g. description, primary UOM).\n- **Org-level attributes** can differ by org (e.g. planner, min-max levels, lead times).\n- This avoids duplicate items and keeps definitions consistent.\n\nIn Fusion the master org is set up in Product Hub, and **item classes** and **templates** default attributes.","B"],["Inventory","What are the main inventory transaction types?","- **Receipts** — from purchase orders or transfers.\n- **Issues** — to work orders, accounts (miscellaneous issue), sales orders (shipping).\n- **Subinventory transfers** — moving stock within an org.\n- **Interorganization transfers** — between orgs (direct or in-transit).\n- **Miscellaneous receipt/issue** — adjustments to an account.\n- **Cycle count and physical inventory adjustments.**\n\nEach has a transaction type, source type and action, and creates cost accounting.","B"],["Inventory","What is lot and serial control?","- **Lot control** tracks a batch of items with the same properties (e.g. made together, same expiry date). Common in pharma and food. Lots can carry expiration dates, grades, status.\n- **Serial control** tracks each individual unit with a unique number (e.g. equipment).\n- Serial control can be at **receipt**, **predefined**, or **at sales order issue**.\n- Both give full traceability: where a lot/serial came from and where it went (genealogy).\n\nControl is set on the item and can't easily be changed once transactions exist.","B"],["Inventory","What is the difference between cycle counting and physical inventory?","- **Cycle counting** — counting a small set of items regularly (often by **ABC classification**: A items counted more often). Keeps records accurate without shutting down operations.\n- **Physical inventory** — counting everything at once (often at year end), usually freezing transactions during the count.\n\n## Cycle count process\n- Define the cycle count, classes and frequency.\n- Generate count schedules and count sequences.\n- Enter counts; **recounts** for out-of-tolerance results.\n- **Approve** adjustments (tolerances drive approval); adjustments post to inventory and accounting.","B"],["Inventory","How does min-max planning work?","- Set **minimum** and **maximum** quantities for an item in an org (or subinventory).\n- The min-max report/process checks on-hand plus on-order. If it falls below the **min**, it suggests (or creates) a replenishment order to bring it up to the **max**.\n- Replenishment can be a requisition (purchase), a transfer from another org, or a work order.\n- Fixed lot multiples and minimum order quantities adjust the suggested quantity.\n\nIt's simple and works well for stable-demand items like spare parts and consumables.","B"],["Inventory","What is a reservation?","A reservation **holds on-hand quantity** for a specific demand (e.g. a sales order or work order) so no one else can use it.\n- Can be at the org, subinventory, lot, locator or serial level.\n- Reduces **available to reserve / available to transact** quantities.\n- Created manually or automatically (e.g. during scheduling).\n\nTip: Difference — **on-hand** = physically there; **available** = on-hand minus reservations and other holds.","D"],["Receiving","What are the receipt routings?","- **Direct delivery** — receive and deliver in one step to the final destination.\n- **Standard receipt** — receive first (to a receiving location), then deliver in a separate step.\n- **Inspection required** — receive, inspect (accept/reject), then deliver.\n\nRouting defaults from the PO schedule, item, supplier or receiving options. The choice affects when inventory becomes available and when accrual accounting happens.","B"],["Receiving","How do you return goods to a supplier?","- Create a **return to supplier** transaction against the receipt (Receiving → Returns).\n- Inventory is reduced; receipt accounting reverses the accrual.\n- If already invoiced, AP needs a **debit memo** (can be automatic with RTS-based debit memos if enabled on the supplier site).\n- Use **RMA numbers** from the supplier and track the return shipment.\n- Corrections (vs returns) fix data entry errors on a receipt rather than physically sending goods back.","B"],["Shipping","What is pick release and what happens during it?","Pick release selects order lines that are ready to ship and creates **pick tasks/move orders**.\n- Uses **release rules**: order, customer, ship date, warehouse, shipment priority.\n- **Picking rules** decide where to pick from (e.g. FIFO, FEFO by lot expiry).\n- It can auto-allocate and auto-pick-confirm.\n- After picking, items move to staging; then pack and **ship confirm** creates the shipment and issues inventory.","B"],["Inventory","What is an interorganization transfer and how is it set up?","Moving inventory between two inventory orgs.\n- **Shipping networks** define the from/to orgs, transfer type (**direct** or **in-transit**), intransit accounts and whether receipt is required.\n- **Direct** — moves instantly.\n- **In-transit** — shipped, sits in transit, then received at the destination.\n- Can use **transfer orders** (Fusion) for a full ship/receive flow with transfer pricing.\n- Cost: transfer price and freight can create intercompany accounting if orgs are in different legal entities.","D"],["Inventory","How do you investigate an on-hand quantity that doesn't match the physical count?","- Check **pending/unprocessed transactions** (interface errors, pending transactions, unprocessed receipts or shipments).\n- Review **transaction history** for the item/subinventory/locator — look for wrong subinventory, unit of measure mistakes, or lot/serial errors.\n- Check **reservations** and staged quantities (picked but not shipped).\n- Recount to confirm.\n- Fix with an **adjustment** (with approval) and identify the root cause (training, process, scanning).","B"],["Receiving","What is the difference between receipt tolerance and invoice tolerance?","- **Receipt tolerances** (quantity, date) control how much over/under or how early/late you can receive vs the PO — with actions like warning or reject.\n- **Invoice tolerances** (price, quantity, amount) in AP control how much an invoice can differ from PO/receipt before a **matching hold** is placed.\n\nThey work together: tight receipt tolerance prevents over-receiving; invoice tolerance prevents overpaying.","B"],["Shipping","What documents are produced during shipping?","- **Pick slip** — what to pick and where.\n- **Packing slip** — what's in the shipment, for the customer.\n- **Bill of lading (BOL)** — contract with the carrier.\n- **Commercial invoice** — for international shipments/customs.\n- **Labels** (shipping, carton).\n- **ASN** (advance shipment notice) — electronic notice to the customer.\n\nThey're produced using document sets at pick release or ship confirm.","B"],["SCM Basics","What is the difference between an inventory organization and a business unit in Fusion SCM?","- **Business unit** — performs business functions like procurement, requisitioning, order fulfillment and billing; has its own setups and data security.\n- **Inventory organization** — a physical location that holds and transacts inventory.\n- An inventory org is tied to a **profit center BU** (and legal entity) for accounting. A procurement BU can serve many requisitioning BUs and inventory orgs.\n\nGetting these relationships right is key in the enterprise structure design.","D"],["Inventory","What is ABC classification and how is it used?","ABC analysis ranks items by value (usually annual usage value = quantity × cost):\n- **A items** — roughly 10–20% of items, ~70–80% of value. Counted most often, tightest control.\n- **B items** — middle.\n- **C items** — many items, little value. Counted least often.\n\nIn Oracle you **compile an ABC analysis** for an org, assign classes, and use them in **cycle count** frequency and planning policies.","B"],["Inventory","What is the difference between a subinventory transfer and an interorganization transfer?","- **Subinventory transfer**: moves stock **within the same inventory org** (e.g. Stores → Staging). No change of owner or cost org; usually no accounting impact unless subinventories are valued differently.\n- **Interorganization transfer**: moves stock **between orgs** (e.g. plant → DC). Needs a shipping network; may be direct or in-transit; creates accounting and possibly intercompany entries.","B"],["Inventory","What is a miscellaneous transaction and when is it used?","- A **miscellaneous receipt or issue** adjusts on-hand against an **account** rather than a document (PO/SO).\n- Uses: initial stock load, write-offs, samples, corrections, scrap.\n- Usually restricted by role and sometimes needs approval, because it changes inventory value directly.","B"],["Receiving","How do you correct a receipt entered with the wrong quantity?","- Use a **receipt correction** (not a return): increase or decrease the quantity received/delivered.\n- If the goods were already delivered to inventory, correct the delivery too.\n- If invoiced, check the AP impact (quantity holds or debit memo).\n- Returns are only for goods physically sent back to the supplier.","B"],["Inventory","How would you run an inventory accuracy improvement program?","- Measure **record accuracy** (by count) and value variance by location.\n- Implement **ABC cycle counting** with tolerances and root-cause codes.\n- Fix **process causes**: receiving delays, unrecorded issues, wrong locators, UOM mistakes.\n- Use **scanning/mobile** transactions instead of paper.\n- **Locator control** and material status to stop misplacement.\n- Weekly review of adjustments with warehouse leads.\n- Target e.g. 98%+ accuracy for A items.","D"],["Inventory","How would you design lot and serial control for a pharmaceutical company?","- **Lot control** on all API, raw materials and finished goods; **serial control** at saleable units if **DSCSA/EU FMD** serialization applies (often in a dedicated serialization system integrated with Oracle).\n- **Lot attributes**: expiry, retest date, manufacturer lot, grade, origin.\n- **Material status**: quarantine → approved/released → rejected; QA release controls availability.\n- **FEFO** picking.\n- **Genealogy** for recall: from raw material lot to customer shipment.\n- **GxP considerations**: validated system (CSV), audit trails, electronic signatures (21 CFR Part 11) for key transactions.","D"],["Inventory","How do you plan the inventory cutover for a warehouse go-live?","- **Freeze** transactions in legacy (stop receiving/shipping or log them manually).\n- **Physical count** (or validated cycle-count snapshot) by item, lot, serial, locator.\n- **Load on-hand** via inventory transaction import with costs from finance; reconcile quantity and value to legacy and GL.\n- Convert **open POs, sales orders, transfers** after on-hand.\n- **Catch-up** manual transactions after go-live.\n- Shipping/receiving restart with floor support.\n\nPlan around low-volume days and coordinate with customers/suppliers.","D"],["Returns","How would you design a returns (RMA) process with inspection and refurbishment?","- **Return orders** referencing original orders; return reasons drive the flow.\n- **Receive** into a returns subinventory with **inspection** routing.\n- Disposition: restock (good), **refurbish** (work order to repair), scrap, or return to vendor.\n- **Credit** created through AR once received/inspected (or immediately for trusted customers).\n- **Cost**: returned items valued at original cost or a refurbishment cost.\n- Analytics on return reasons to fix root causes.","D"],["Logistics","When would you recommend a WMS over standard Fusion Inventory?","- **High volume** DCs (many lines/day), complex picking (waves, zones, cartonization).\n- **LPN-based** operations, cross-docking, directed putaway.\n- **Labor management** and automation (conveyors, robots).\n- **Mobile RF** throughout.\n\nFusion Warehouse Management covers many of these natively now; best-of-breed WMS (Manhattan, Blue Yonder) for very large or automated DCs. Decision: volume, complexity, automation, and cost to integrate.","D"],["Inventory","How do you set up material status control for quarantine and QA release?","- Define statuses (e.g. **Quarantine**, **Approved**, **Rejected**, **Hold**) with **allowed transactions** (e.g. Quarantine: no shipping, no issue to production, allows transfer to QA lab).\n- Assign default status at **subinventory**, **locator**, **lot** or **serial** level.\n- QA changes status after inspection (with electronic signature in regulated environments).\n- Picking/ATP respects status.\n- Reports of material in quarantine with ageing.","D"],["Receiving","How would you speed up receiving at a busy distribution center?","- Require **ASNs** from top suppliers so receipts are pre-populated.\n- **Barcode/mobile receiving** with LPN or lot scanning.\n- **Direct delivery** routing for low-risk items; inspection only where needed.\n- **Receiving tolerances** that auto-accept small differences.\n- **Dock scheduling** to level the workload.\n- Measure dock-to-stock time and receipt backlog daily.","B"],["Inventory","How would you reduce cycle count adjustments at a DC?","- Analyze adjustments by item, locator, user and reason.\n- Fix root causes: receiving delays, picking errors, UOM confusion, unrecorded scrap.\n- **Scanning** and locator control.\n- Tighten tolerances for A items; recount before approval.\n- Weekly review with warehouse leads.","D"],["Receiving","What is an ASN and how is it used in receiving?","An **Advance Shipment Notice** tells you what a supplier has shipped (items, quantities, lots/serials, expected date) before it arrives.\n- Created by the supplier in the portal or via B2B (EDI 856).\n- Receivers **receive against the ASN** — faster and more accurate.\n- **ASBN** (advance shipment and billing notice) can also create the invoice.\n- Can be required on the PO (ASN reference required) to force suppliers to send them.","B"],["Receiving","How does receipt accounting create accruals and how do you clear old accruals?","- On delivery to inventory or expense (accrue at receipt), Receipt Accounting books **Dr Inventory/Expense, Cr Receipt Accrual**.\n- When the invoice is matched in AP, AP books **Dr Accrual, Cr Liability**; differences go to IPV/variance accounts.\n- Use **Accrual Reconciliation** in Receipt Accounting (Review Receipt Accounting Distributions, accrual balances report) to find accruals with no invoice or mismatches.\n- **Write off** with approval or **clear** after investigation (e.g. PO should be closed).\n- Close POs regularly to stop accruals building up.","D"],["Inventory","What is consigned inventory and how is it handled in Fusion?","**Supplier consignment**: the supplier owns the inventory sitting in your warehouse until you consume it.\n- A **consignment agreement** (BPA with consignment terms) with the supplier.\n- Receipts go into inventory as **consigned** (owned by the supplier) — no liability.\n- When you **consume** it (issue, transfer, or after aging), ownership transfers: a **consumption advice** is created and AP invoices the supplier (or pay-on-use).\n- On-hand shows both owned and consigned quantities.\n\nCustomer consignment (your stock at a customer site) is the reverse.","D"],["Inventory","How do you set up and use transfer orders in Fusion?","- **Transfer orders** move inventory between orgs with a full order flow: request → approve → ship → receive.\n- Created from **transfer requests**, planning, min-max, or the internal material transfer UI.\n- Sourced by **sourcing rules**; shipping network and **transfer pricing** (cost or price list) defined.\n- Can create intercompany accounting via SFO if orgs belong to different legal entities.\n- Track status from the Transfer Orders work area.","D"],["Inventory","How do you configure cycle counting with approvals and tolerances?","- Create the **cycle count** for an org with subinventories, classes (ABC) and frequency.\n- **Count tolerances**: quantity % and value thresholds — within tolerance post automatically, otherwise **recount** or **approve**.\n- **Approval required**: always, never, or only if out of tolerance.\n- **Count sequences** generated by schedules; counters enter counts (mobile/web).\n- **Adjustments** post with an account (cycle count adjustment account) and create cost accounting.","D"],["Inventory","What is the difference between available to transact, available to reserve and on-hand?","- **On-hand** — total physical quantity in the location.\n- **Available to reserve** — on-hand minus existing reservations (and some statuses).\n- **Available to transact** — what you can actually move or issue now — excludes quantities that are reserved, in non-transactable subinventories, or with restrictive **material status** (e.g. lot on hold).\n\nMaterial status (at subinventory, locator, lot, serial) controls which transactions are allowed — e.g. a quarantine status blocks shipping.","B"],["Shipping","How do you configure shipping for a new warehouse?","- **Shipping parameters** for the org (pick release defaults, staging subinventory, auto-pack, ship confirm rules).\n- **Picking rules** (FIFO/FEFO, locator order).\n- **Release rules and release sequence rules.**\n- **Document sets** (pick slips, packing slips, BOL) and printers.\n- **Carriers and ship methods**, freight terms, shipping costs.\n- **Shipping exceptions** and their actions.\n- Test pick → pack → ship confirm → invoicing.","D"],["Shipping","What are shipping exceptions?","Exceptions flag problems during shipping — e.g. **short pick**, damaged goods, missing documents.\n- Defined with a severity (error, warning, info) and handling.\n- Can be logged manually or automatically.\n- Errors block ship confirm until resolved.\n- Reports help track recurring warehouse problems.","B"],["Logistics","What does Oracle Transportation Management (OTM) add to Fusion SCM?","OTM handles **transportation planning and execution**:\n- Load building and **route optimization**.\n- **Carrier selection** and **rate management**.\n- **Tendering** to carriers and tracking.\n- **Freight payment and audit**.\n- Integrates with Fusion Shipping (shipments go to OTM; planned shipments come back).\n\nUseful for high-volume shippers to reduce freight cost.","D"],["Logistics","What is Warehouse Management (WMS) and how is it different from standard Inventory?","- Standard Inventory tracks quantities by org/subinventory/locator.\n- **WMS** adds **task-based** warehouse operations: directed putaway, wave picking, **LPNs** (license plate numbers), cartonization, cross-docking, labor management, mobile RF devices.\n- Fusion now has **Warehouse Management** built on Inventory, and many companies use specialized WMS (Manhattan, Blue Yonder) integrated via APIs.\n\nLPN = a container ID (pallet/case) you transact as a unit.","D"],["Inventory","How do you handle lot expiry and FEFO in Oracle?","- Enable **lot control** with **shelf life** (days) on the item; lots get an expiration date at receipt.\n- **Picking rules** with **FEFO** (first expired, first out) allocate the nearest-expiring lots first.\n- **Material status** can block expired lots from being shipped.\n- Reports/alerts for lots near expiry; lot **grade** and **retest dates** for pharma.\n- Expired lots: move to a quarantine subinventory or scrap with approval.","D"],["SCM Basics","What setups must exist before you can create an inventory organization in Fusion?","- **Location** (address).\n- **Legal entity** and **business unit** (profit center BU).\n- **Item master organization**.\n- **Calendar** (workday calendar for scheduling).\n- **Cost organization** relationship (for costing).\n- **Ledger** association via legal entity/BU.\n\nThen: inventory org parameters, subinventories, locators, shipping/receiving parameters, and cost setups.","B"],["Inventory","What are the key inventory organization parameters you set?","- **Item master org**, schedule (calendar), **default subinventory/locator controls**.\n- **Lot and serial** number generation and uniqueness.\n- **Negative balances allowed** or not.\n- **Picking and putaway** defaults (picking rule, allocation).\n- **ATP and reservations** settings.\n- **Shipping/receiving** parameters, interorg transfer defaults.\n- **Costing**: linked cost org, default accounts.\n\nDecisions like negative balances need business agreement — they affect controls and costing.","D"],["Inventory","How do you handle negative on-hand in an org that allows negative balances?","- It usually means issues were recorded before receipts (timing) or a receipt was missed.\n- Find the item/locations negative, check pending receipts and transfers, process them.\n- Costing: negative quantities can cause cost adjustments later — monitor.\n- Consider disallowing negative balances once processes are stable.","D"],["Inventory","How would you reduce excess and obsolete inventory using the platform?","- **Visibility**: aging, days of supply, slow movers by item/location.\n- **Root causes**: forecast bias, MOQ/lot sizes, lifecycle changes not communicated.\n- **Planning policies**: adjust safety stock, order modifiers; phase-out planning for end-of-life items.\n- **Disposition** workflows (rebalance across DCs, sell, return to vendor, scrap).\n- **Reserves** policy with finance.\n- KPIs and monthly review.","D"],["Logistics","How do you approach freight cost reduction through systems?","- **Visibility**: freight cost by lane, carrier, mode, customer.\n- **TMS optimization**: load consolidation, mode selection, routing.\n- **Carrier procurement** with rate analytics.\n- **Freight audit and pay** to catch billing errors.\n- **Order policies**: minimum order quantities, delivery days.\n- **Network design** studies.","D"]]);
